Moving from left to right, we first see customers with operational data stores for their applications. These data stores are often relational but also semi-structured with documents or key/value pair storage. These stores are optimized for application performance with millisecond response times and high-availability being top concerns.
The next type of store is a relational data warehouse. This is where disparate data is brought together for analysis. A data warehouse helps customers answer questions about their business with a holistic view of the organization.
Many organizations have also embraced data lakes with structured, semi-structure, and even unstructured data. It too brings all of the data in the organization together for analysis but it expands the scope of the data available. 
Lastly, we have the data mesh architecture which is an architectural framework that solves advanced data security challenges through distributed, decentralized ownership. Organizations have multiple data sources from different lines of business that must be integrated for analytics. Business functions can maintain control over how shared data is accessed, who accesses it, and in what formats it’s accessed. 


Pattern 1 is frequently used by applications. This is where existing data is enhanced by creating vector embeddings for similarity searches. These vector embeddings are created by large language models like Amazon Titan and can be stored along side the relational and/or semi-structured data found in SQL and NOSQL data stores respectively.
Pattern 2 is used for analytical workloads found in data warehouses and data lakes. 
Pattern 3 is designing for Unified Lakehouse that includes unified data catalog across data warehouse, data lake and federated sources - and accessible to a variety of analytics engines via open Iceberg compatible APIs
Pattern 4 is the evolution of these pattern to a data mesh architecture.

Pattern 1; where you store vectors alongside your application data.

A customer may have an existing application where they want to add Generative AI capabilities with similarity searches. A vector embedding makes it possible to understand the meaning of text with similarity comparisons of vectors generated from LLMs. These databases are typically indexed for optimal performance including indexing the vector data. 

As our customers use vectors for their generative AI applications, they told us that they want to use them with their existing databases to eliminate the learning curve for new programming tools, APIs, SDKs.

They also feel more confident knowing their existing databases are proven in production and meet requirements for scalability, availability, storage and compute. 

And when your vectors and business data are stored in the same place, your applications will run faster – and there’s no data sync or data movement to worry about. 

For all of these reasons, we’ve invested in adding vector capabilities to some of our most popular data services.

Now, for those in the audience unfamiliar with semantic context and how that gets derived, here’s a short primer on vector embeddings.

You take your domain-specific data, and break it apart into elements that hold individual meaning in a process called tokenization. 

The elements can be words, phrases, paragraphs, entire documents – whatever makes sense for your use case, such that you can have a set of meaningfully composable elements. You take the elements and pass them through an LLM to derive numerical vectors – essentially arrays of numbers for each element. These vectors place elements with related meaning close to each other in a multi-dimensional vector space. 

Thus, finding meaningfully or semantically related data is reduced to mathematical functions of minimal distance between elements.

When you are using vector elements to represent meaning, in the context of generative AI, these are referred to as “embeddings”. 

