A conventional data pipeline with the typical ingestion, processing, integration, transformation and governance components. 

Lets see how the scope of some of the simple data pipeline components is influenced when you build a generative AI application. 

Existing data pipeline data can still be used by GenAI application


One of the very first things that gen AI introduces to the pipeline is the need for additional data sources. And these are primarily in the form of unstructured data. Now, unstructured data is not a new construct to the data world, however it is now the dominant part of the data feed for a gen AI pipeline. Our typical data architecture patterns have largely dealt with structured data, but with more than 80% of the world’s data in unstructured formats, it is now front and center with gen AI, and has a big influence on data ingestion, data integration and data governance. 
Unstructured data is information that doesn’t conform to a predefined schema or isn’t organized according to a preset data model. Understanding the data, categorizing it, and extracting insights from it can be challenging. Metadata discovery is one of the primary requirements in data analytics.

Customers may be using call transcripts/PDFs etc …so you were not able to derive insights without GenAI

You could be working with feature engineering to extract information from data in your data warehouse or data lake, to represent it in a way that makes it easier for ML algorithms to learn from. 
Or you could be running inference for your continued pre-training approach to draw new conclusions for a more effective response from your gen AI application. 
Or managing vector data for a RAG based application to provide real-time, meaningful context.
All this processing requires advanced forms of data integration and data stores. 

Some gen AI applications also incorporate advanced learning techniques such as Reinforcement learning from human feedback (RLHF), a machine learning (ML) technique that uses human feedback to optimize ML models. Similarly, these applications will also need to capture user input data in real time for a highly personalized and accurate response. Capturing this information in the right kind of data stores with efficient pipelines helps with latency and accuracy.


Pattern 4
So data architecture pattern lands the data in data lake house. Customers may have many applications and data owners who do not know how and who to share data with, similarly there may be many consumer groups/departments who do now know what data is available, who to submit request to access the data and there are questions about standards and quality of data available. So data architecture pattern 3 intends to address these requirements using data mesh architecture.

Data Sharing is at the core of this data architecture pattern - Using data sharing, your gen AI applications can also access 100s of data products within their own organizations and from AWS data market place that are published by third party providers like equinox, s&p global, factset

Your data producers populate the business data catalog with data assets and also enrich them with business context for data consumers to understand. Your consumers such as gen AI applications can search and access this data by requesting subscriptions. When the request is approved, they can start querying the data

Data governance has multiple dimensions including building a technical data catalog with information on data format, partitioning, location, format and more. 

While using your data to customize gen AI applications, you need to ensure that privacy and compliance requirements are met. You also need to ensure that your gen AI applications can easily find the data they need. Data governance frameworks help with that. 

So using data mesh, customers enable self-serving data culture within their organizations.








